ranks 5th lowest in the league.","special":"The Ravens have been one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 14% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers have made 78% of field goals, which is a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the quarterback, about 21% of the money on the books. Their defensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"The quarterback job has been split, with Tyler Huntley and Lamar Jackson both getting meaningful snaps. Zay Flowers has been the clear top target with 27% of the looks and 614 receiving yards. Derrick Henry has handled most of the work in the backfield with 79% of the carries. On defense, Mike Green leads the team with 3.5 sacks.","coaching":"Declan Doyle is calling the offense and Jesse Minter is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2023 Bears and the 2022 Titans. Their defense looks most like the 2023 Chargers under Brandon Staley and the 2023 Jaguars.","trends":"The offense went from 12th in EPA per play in 2025 to 18th so far this season, a modest decline. The defense moved from 20th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 19th so far this season, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was number of pass rushers, which went from 4.23 to 4.45.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}